---
// /new/link/: the mirror mode (D00-2). A repository the person already has on GitHub, public, which
// they administer or maintain, linked to the registry and to its papers in one authorized action
// (E1): with the App when one of their installations covers it (its pushes followed as they
// happen, by webhooks), without it otherwise (read only, read every night). The registry never
// copies the repository: git stays on GitHub.
//
// The form is shown by its script (src/scripts/link-repo.ts, a file: the page's
// Content-Security-Policy forbids inline scripts), pre-filled from ?repo=owner/name and ?paper=DOI;
// after an installation of the App (/forge/authorized/ links here with ?installation=ID) a note says
// so. science.css only; the platform's name from src/config.ts.
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../../config";

const LINKS = {
  apps: "https://docs.github.com/en/apps/using-github-apps/about-using-github-apps",
  installed: "https://github.com/settings/installations",
  roles: "https://docs.github.com/en/organizations/managing-user-access-to-your-organizations-repositories/managing-repository-roles/repository-roles-for-an-organization",
};
---
<Base
  title="Link a repository"
  description="Link a GitHub repository you already have to your paper: the mirror mode."
  breadcrumb={[{ text: "Repositories", href: "/repositories/" }, { text: "Link a repository" }]}
>
  <h1>Link a repository</h1>
  <p class="summary">
    A repository you already have on GitHub, linked to your paper: with the App installed, its pushes are followed as
    they happen; without it, it is read every night. It stays where it is, in your account; {SITE_NAME} copies nothing
    of it but what its licence allows.
  </p>
  <noscript>
    <p class="warning">This form needs JavaScript: it asks GitHub for your authorization, then comes back here.</p>
  </noscript>
  <p id="link-installed" class="ok" hidden>
    The App is installed on GitHub. Name one of the repositories you gave it, below, to link it.
  </p>
  <p id="link-message" role="status"></p>

  <form id="link-form" hidden>
    <p>
      <label for="link-repository">The repository</label>: owner/name, or its GitHub address<br />
      <input type="text" id="link-repository" name="repository" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" maxlength="300" placeholder="lab/eeg-analysis" required />
    </p>
    <p>
      <label for="link-papers">Its papers</label> (DOIs, separated by spaces; optional)<br />
      <input type="text" id="link-papers" name="papers" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" maxlength="2000" placeholder="10.5555/…" />
    </p>
    <p><label><input type="checkbox" name="install" /> Install the App on it first</label> (if it is not installed yet)</p>
    <p><button type="submit">Link</button></p>
    <div id="link-confirm" aria-live="polite"></div>
  </form>

  <h2>Who may link it</h2>
  <p>
    A person who administers or maintains the repository on GitHub (<a href={LINKS.roles}>repository roles</a>): GitHub
    says so when you authorize. A private repository is never linked, and its name is kept nowhere. A paper you are a
    verified author of is linked at once; another is proposed to its authors.
  </p>

  <h2>With the App, or without it</h2>
  <dl class="settings">
    <dt>With the App</dt>
    <dd>
      GitHub tells {SITE_NAME} of each push, rename, archive or deletion as it happens (a webhook, signed by GitHub and
      checked), so the repository's page and its tracing maps follow at once. The App may read the repositories you
      choose when you install it, and later post its checks on pull requests. It never pushes code, never reads a
      private repository you did not give it, and you remove it at any time from
      <a href={LINKS.installed}>your GitHub settings</a> (<a href={LINKS.apps}>about GitHub Apps</a>).
    </dd>
    <dt>Without it</dt>
    <dd>
      The repository is read only, the way anyone reads a public repository: {SITE_NAME} checks its head every night,
      so its page may be a day behind.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>What linking does not do</h2>
  <p>
    It moves nothing and copies no history: git goes on to GitHub as before, with your own credentials. Refs such as
    pull requests' (refs/pull/…) are never mirrored. To bring code from another host, <a href="/new/import/">import
    it</a> first; to start a new repository, <a href="/new/">create one</a>.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/repositories/">Your repositories</a>.</li>
    <li><a href="/hosting/">The hosting guides</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <script>
    import "../../scripts/link-repo";
  </script>
</Base>
